---
import Docs from '../../layouts/Docs.astro';
import Cmd from '../../components/ui/Cmd.astro';
import Callout from '../../components/docs/Callout.astro';
import { SITE } from '../../site';

const TOC = [
  { id: 'prerequisites', label: 'Prerequisites' },
  { id: 'install', label: 'Install' },
  { id: 'first-run', label: 'First run' },
  { id: 'flags', label: 'Installer flags' },
  { id: 'try-it-on-a-real-bug', label: 'Try it on a real bug' },
  { id: 'providers-that-work', label: 'Providers that work' },
  { id: 'upgrades', label: 'Upgrades' },
  { id: 'exposing-it', label: 'Exposing it' },
];

const FLAGS = [
  { flag: '--workspace DIR', meaning: 'Create this directory and use it as the first project.' },
  { flag: '--version VER', meaning: 'Release to install instead of the latest, e.g. 0.1.0.' },
  { flag: '--prefix DIR', meaning: 'Install the binary to DIR/bin. Default /usr/local.' },
  { flag: '--no-systemd', meaning: 'Binary only: no unit, no service user, no root needed.' },
  { flag: '--data-dir DIR', meaning: 'SQLite database and plugin data. Default ~/.local/share/karucode.' },
  { flag: '--port N', meaning: 'Listen port. Default 8787.' },
  { flag: '--dry-run', meaning: 'Print every action and change nothing. Works without root.' },
];

const PROVIDERS = [
  { name: 'LiteLLM proxy', url: 'http://127.0.0.1:4000/v1' },
  { name: 'vLLM', url: 'http://127.0.0.1:8000/v1' },
  { name: 'Ollama', url: 'http://127.0.0.1:11434/v1' },
  { name: 'llama.cpp server', url: 'http://127.0.0.1:8080/v1' },
  { name: 'OpenRouter', url: 'https://openrouter.ai/api/v1' },
];
---

<Docs
  title="Quick start"
  description="Install karucode from the verified release tarball, point it at an OpenAI-compatible endpoint, and give it a task."
  path="/docs/quickstart/"
  section="Docs"
  lede="Install the binary, point it at a model, run a task."
  toc={TOC}
  edit={`${SITE.repo}/blob/main/README.md`}
  prev={{ href: '/docs/', title: 'Overview' }}
  next={{ href: '/docs/configuration/', title: 'Configuration' }}
>
  <p>
    The release is a prebuilt binary with the UI inside it, so there is nothing to compile. What you
    need is a machine that can reach an OpenAI-compatible endpoint — a local gateway, a hosted API,
    or a model server you already run.
  </p>

  <h2 id="prerequisites">Prerequisites</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Linux, x86_64</strong> — the target the release is built for.</li>
    <li>
      <strong><code>curl</code> and <code>sh</code></strong> — the installer is a POSIX shell script.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>systemd</strong> for the service install. Without it, use
      <code>--no-systemd</code> and run the binary yourself.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>No Rust and no Node.</strong> Those are only needed to build from source, which is a
      contributor workflow, not an install path.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    That is the whole list. There is no cloud account, no signup and no telemetry: the binary talks
    to the endpoint you give it and to nothing else.
  </p>

  <h2 id="install">Install</h2>
  <p>
    Download the installer, then run it. It fetches the release tarball, verifies it against the
    release’s <code>SHA256SUMS</code>, and only then unpacks and installs — nothing is piped from the
    network into a shell.
  </p>
  <Cmd
    label="shell"
    prompt=""
    wrap
    command={`curl -fsSLO https://raw.githubusercontent.com/karutoil/karucode/main/packaging/install.sh
sudo sh install.sh --workspace /srv/karucode/workspace`}
  />
  <p>
    It installs <code>/usr/local/bin/karucode</code>, writes a hardened systemd unit plus a drop-in
    with this machine’s paths, enables it at boot, starts it, and waits until
    <code>/api/health</code> answers before reporting success. The previous binary is kept as
    <code>karucode.old</code>; a failed start restores it. <code>--dry-run</code> prints the whole
    plan without changing anything.
  </p>
  <Callout kind="note" title="No root? Same script.">
    <p>
      <code>sh install.sh --prefix "$HOME/.local" --no-systemd</code> puts the binary in
      <code>~/.local/bin</code> and leaves the service alone. Run it in the foreground with
      <code>karucode --workspace /path/to/project</code>.
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <h2 id="first-run">First run</h2>
  <p>
    Open <a href="http://127.0.0.1:8787">http://127.0.0.1:8787</a>. The first-run wizard takes the
    rest in six steps: the model endpoint (with a live “Test connection”), appearance, approval mode,
    and a password or passkey.
  </p>
  <p>
    The endpoint is a prefix, not a full URL: karucode appends <code>/chat/completions</code> to it.
    Point it at the <code>/v1</code> root of your gateway, not at a single endpoint. The same three
    values can be set from the environment instead — <code>KARUCODE_BASE_URL</code>,
    <code>KARUCODE_API_KEY</code> (it falls back to <code>OPENAI_API_KEY</code>) and
    <code>KARUCODE_MODEL</code> — which is what a sealed deployment does.
  </p>
  <p>
    The default approval mode is <code>ask</code>: shell commands and file writes wait for you. You
    can answer allow-once, allow-for-session or deny, and every decision is recorded. A password or
    passkey is optional on loopback, and required before you expose the UI.
  </p>

  <h2 id="flags">Installer flags</h2>
  <p>
    Every flag is optional except that the systemd path needs root and a prefix it can write.
    <code>sh install.sh --help</code> prints the full list.
  </p>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Flag</th>
          <th scope="col">Meaning</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {
          FLAGS.map((f) => (
            <tr>
              <td>{f.flag}</td>
              <td>{f.meaning}</td>
            </tr>
          ))
        }
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2 id="try-it-on-a-real-bug">Try it on a real bug</h2>
  <p>
    Point it at a project you already have — a checkout with a failing test is the honest first task.
    In the UI, add the project, start a session and describe the bug. If you want a known case,
    <code>examples/demo-app</code> in the repository is a dependency-free Node project with a planted
    bug: <code>add()</code> subtracts instead of adding, so two of its five tests fail.
  </p>
  <Cmd label="shell" wrap command="karucode --workspace examples/demo-app" />
  <p>Then give it this prompt:</p>
  <blockquote>
    <p>
      Run the tests, find the failing cases, fix the bug in src/math.js, and re-run the tests to prove
      it passes.
    </p>
  </blockquote>
  <p>
    Watch what it does: it runs the tests, reads the failures, opens the file, edits one function, and
    runs the tests again. You will be asked before the first command and before the write. That
    loop — run, read, change, prove — is the whole product.
  </p>

  <h2 id="providers-that-work">Providers that work</h2>
  <p>
    Anything that speaks the OpenAI chat-completions API with streaming tool calls. These are the
    shapes people point it at most often:
  </p>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Provider</th>
          <th scope="col">KARUCODE_BASE_URL</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {
          PROVIDERS.map((p) => (
            <tr>
              <td>{p.name}</td>
              <td>
                <code>{p.url}</code>
              </td>
            </tr>
          ))
        }
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    With Ollama, tool calling depends on the model you pull — a model that cannot emit tool calls
    cannot drive the agent. Provider profiles are install-wide; a session can pin its own model,
    provider and reasoning effort. See <a href="/docs/configuration/">Configuration</a>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="upgrades">Upgrades</h2>
  <p>
    Re-running the installer is the upgrade — it fetches the latest release and restarts the service:
  </p>
  <Cmd label="shell" wrap command="sudo sh install.sh" />
  <p>
    Your workspace, service user, port and data directory live in a drop-in
    (<code>/etc/systemd/system/karucode.service.d/10-install.conf</code>) that every run rewrites from
    what it finds, so an upgrade cannot lose the paths the service needs. Pin a version with
    <code>--version 0.1.0</code>, or leave it on <code>latest</code>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="exposing-it">Exposing it</h2>
  <Callout kind="danger" title="Not for a public interface as-is">
    <p>
      karucode binds to <code>127.0.0.1</code> by default and refuses a non-loopback bind unless
      <code>KARUCODE_AUTH_TOKEN</code> is set (16+ characters) or sign-in is enabled with a
      password or passkey. Sign-in is strongly recommended before the UI leaves loopback. There is
      no TLS in the binary: put a reverse proxy in front of it for remote access.
    </p>
    <p>
      Shell commands run as the server user and are not sandboxed. If the workspace is untrusted, run
      the whole thing inside a container or a VM. <a href="/security/">Security</a> is the full
      version of this page.
    </p>
  </Callout>
</Docs>

<style>
  .table-wrap code {
    font-size: 0.8125rem;
  }

  /* At phone width the URL column is the only thing that doesn't fit; let it
     break rather than hide half of it behind a horizontal scroll. */
  @media (max-width: 560px) {
    .table-wrap :global(code) {
      font-size: 0.75rem;
      white-space: normal;
      overflow-wrap: anywhere;
    }

    .table-wrap :global(td) {
      padding: 10px 12px;
    }
  }
</style>